1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Music.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,180
Can you hear me now?

3
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:44,540
Good morning.

4
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:46,960
By name.

5
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:47,580
Okay.

6
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:51,980
Well, I'd like to welcome all of you, not just those who welcome me by name, but thank

7
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:52,160
you.

8
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:56,820
I'd like to welcome all of you to this, our second week in the money series.

9
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:02,940
And for those of you who just felt the room kind of go down and are asking yourselves

10
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:07,080
if you're new or if you missed last week, if you're wondering why we're talking about

11
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:12,780
money instead of something spiritual, I would say to you that it's true that our priorities

12
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:18,940
and our outlook in money matters brightly reflect and are strongly influenced by our orientation

13
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:23,120
and our focus and maturity in spiritual matters.

14
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:26,020
One reflects the other clearly, one drives the other clearly.

15
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:29,360
And for those of you who've been around church for a while, you know, that's reflected.

16
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:34,600
I think Ryan said it last week in the common statement that Christ talked more about money

17
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:35,980
matters than any other topic.

18
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:37,400
If you've probably heard that.

19
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:38,840
Yes.

20
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:39,400
Okay.

21
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,000
And for you, I would say that is true.

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,260
Christ did talk about money a lot.

23
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:48,340
And it's important that we understand why he's talking about money and what he's talking

24
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:52,400
about when he talks about money, because he also talked about farming and fishing a lot.

25
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:59,020
And we should not take from that that he particularly cares about how we farm and fish or if we're

26
00:01:59,020 --> 00:01:59,980
farmers and fishermen.

27
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:03,820
We should take from that that he was talking to farmers and fishermen, right?

28
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:08,980
And he was speaking in terms that they would understand, speaking their language to speak

29
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:13,140
to them about spiritual matters and how they should live their life in response to those

30
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:14,360
spiritual truths, right?

31
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:19,780
So when we hear Christ talking about money a lot, I would say to you, what we should take

32
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:26,300
from that is that he talks so much about money to help us realize, or at least we should

33
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:33,020
realize how very focused on money we are, maybe more than him, how much he needs to speak in

34
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:37,160
those terms in order to get our attention and to address our affections.

35
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:43,640
Because, of course, much of our attention is expended in seeking to gather and maximize

36
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:44,160
wealth.

37
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:51,700
We convert our skills and resources, any opportunities that become available to us, we want to turn

38
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:57,240
those into some kind of liquid asset that we need for flourishing today and dealing with

39
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,760
that crisis we fear is going to happen tomorrow, right?

40
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:08,200
We call that temporary storehouse of wealth money, and it's a primary preoccupation of both survival

41
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,200
and flourishing for most of us.

42
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:17,620
Unfortunate part of this is that absorbed in my pursuit of money, my family needs that money

43
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:22,460
for today, and I fear we'll need even more tomorrow because I'm obliged to make comparisons

44
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:23,920
and trade-offs in value.

45
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:30,240
How much am I willing to give up of my time to get my children the thing that I'm pretty sure

46
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,440
will give them the opportunities that they need?

47
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:37,740
How much of my possessions am I willing to forego so that we can have the health care I think

48
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:39,560
we might need tomorrow, right?

49
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:46,660
And these trade-offs in value become a measurement of our life.

50
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:49,120
We have to have some method for making that trade-off.

51
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:55,320
We make that evaluation in money, and imperceptibly, it becomes not just a measurement of our value,

52
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,220
but a ruler of what we value.

53
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:05,280
So sometimes when Christ talks about money, he is using it as an analogy to teach us some

54
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:07,560
other truth, like he does with farming or fishing.

55
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:12,140
And sometimes when he's talking about money, as in the story we're going to deal with today,

56
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:20,780
he's talking to us about what we value and what he values, knowing that in some degree,

57
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:23,880
the answer that we give him would be in terms of money.

58
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:27,480
That's the language we're going to speak in when he says, what do you value?

59
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:33,400
His frequent references to money then, just to repeat it, may reflect our intense interest

60
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:35,620
in the subject more than his, right?

61
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,500
It's a subject then that we need to find his perspective on.

62
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,960
What's the angle that he sees these money questions from?

63
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:47,600
And we'd like to find that today as we look through the story of the rich young ruler.

64
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,700
So turn with me, if you will, to Mark 10.

65
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:52,980
It's going to be up on the screens.

66
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:58,820
The rich young ruler is a story that's repeated in all three of the synoptic Gospels, Matthew, Mark, and Luke.

67
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:00,700
We're going to read it today out of Mark.

68
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:07,660
In Mark 10 then, as he, Jesus, was setting out on a journey, a man ran up to him and knelt before him

69
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:11,000
and asked him, good teacher, what shall I do to inherit eternal life?

70
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,700
And Jesus said to him, why do you call me good?

71
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:15,980
I mean, no one's good except God alone.

72
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,340
You know the commandments.

73
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:19,100
Don't murder.

74
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:20,180
Don't commit adultery.

75
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:20,880
Don't steal.

76
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:22,300
Don't bear false witness.

77
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,200
Don't defraud, which means don't commit fraud.

78
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:26,400
Honor your father and mother.

79
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:31,460
And he said to Jesus, teacher, I've kept all these things from my youth up.

80
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:34,740
And looking up at him, Jesus felt a love for him.

81
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:40,900
And he said to him, one thing you lack, go and sell all you possess and give it to the poor

82
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:42,600
and you'll have treasure in heaven.

83
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:43,800
And come, follow me.

84
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:53,460
But at these words, he was saddened and he went away grieving for he was one who owned much property.

85
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:57,440
It's a very sad story.

86
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,020
The whole room got very quiet.

87
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:04,100
That's a story that's in all three synoptic Gospels.

88
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:07,820
It's one that I can tell the room's not wholly, you know, comfortable with.

89
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:15,600
And it's one that all three authors felt was absolutely core to explaining who Jesus was and what he taught, right?

90
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:18,020
When we see a story that's repeated in all three Gospels,

91
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:24,080
if you don't know, the three synoptic Gospels present the story of who Jesus was and what he taught to different audiences.

92
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:27,820
And so as a result, they will emphasize different points.

93
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:31,700
They'll bring up different stories sometimes that are more relevant to their audience to make a point.

94
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:38,240
Or they'll use wording that, you know, brings out certain details more because they're trying to convey who Jesus was to a new set of people.

95
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,440
And we've collected all three of these in the Bible.

96
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:48,440
So when you see a story that's in all three, it's important to know that's a core one, right?

97
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:54,660
Everybody who hung out with Jesus for the three years he was going around teaching recognized that without this story,

98
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:57,980
you couldn't really convey to everyone what Jesus said and who he was.

99
00:06:58,040 --> 00:06:59,080
So this is a core piece.

100
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:04,500
So when you find those, it's important to see what's the same in all three and what's different, right?

101
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,740
So that you can see what's emphasized to those audiences.

102
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:12,580
The thing that's the same is that this story and its surrounding facts are very, very similar in all three.

103
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:16,500
They keep almost the same wording because this was such a core moment.

104
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:20,020
And we need to ask ourselves why the people who hung out with Jesus thought,

105
00:07:20,020 --> 00:07:22,600
if you're going to understand him, you need to know this story, right?

106
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:25,960
And then there's small things that are different.

107
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:29,960
One thing that's different in that story of the rich young ruler that I just read,

108
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:33,680
did anyone hear the mention of youth or ruling?

109
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:36,260
No, you didn't.

110
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:37,120
There's none.

111
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:38,900
It's not mentioned there.

112
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:40,840
It's just a rich guy came up to Jesus.

113
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:48,200
In Matthew, which was written to a Jewish audience who had Jewish inheritance laws,

114
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:51,380
Matthew, just to add a little color to the story,

115
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:54,480
when he refers the pronoun, he refers to the young man, he says,

116
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:57,100
young man, which is the only way we know it was a young man.

117
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:59,100
And that would have been relevant to the Jewish audience

118
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:02,920
because a young man who had inherited already and had that much wealth

119
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,460
would have had a different outlook on life.

120
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:07,480
And it's significant then that he's asking Jesus,

121
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:10,340
how do I inherit into the kingdom of God, right?

122
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:13,700
Because he's a young man, that means he's just inherited into his family.

123
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:17,660
And he's trying to provide for his family.

124
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:20,400
And he's trying to make sure that they can get into the next step.

125
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:21,920
How can they get into the kingdom of heaven?

126
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:23,340
How can they inherit eternal life?

127
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:25,480
So he's not just thinking about himself.

128
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:28,660
That's just a little color that Matthew throws in for his audience.

129
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:33,060
Luke was writing to people in Rome, the elite of the empire,

130
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:37,500
who were pretty sure that there's nothing significant going on down in Judea.

131
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,420
No one there is significant in any of the stories that they would read in the Bible.

132
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:47,200
As a result, Luke needs to express that this guy coming up to Jesus was a significant person

133
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,660
so that the Romans can understand that when Jesus sort of sends him away

134
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:56,600
and tells him, you're not ready to follow me until you do these things,

135
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:01,560
that they understand that it was an odd and surprising thing that Christ did.

136
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:05,340
And so they say, Luke adds, this young man was a ruler.

137
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:10,080
And when he says ruler, he doesn't mean a governor or king.

138
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:12,140
He means a community leader, right?

139
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:13,640
That's the context it's set in.

140
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:16,560
So we get from what Matthew said and what Luke said,

141
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:19,500
that this man, this wealthy man, is at a young age,

142
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:21,340
a possessor now of the family fortune,

143
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:24,000
and he's risen to some prominence probably in the synagogue.

144
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:26,160
He's one of the local leaders in his community.

145
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:31,300
And as such, he would have had responsibility for those around him.

146
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:35,560
He would have had significance to how he laid out his money.

147
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:39,080
And so Christ telling him to sell all you have and give it to the poor,

148
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:41,480
is Christ asking him in his mind, at least,

149
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:45,140
and somewhat in reality, to dispossess his family, right?

150
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:49,160
He's asking him to get rid of the money you're planning to use

151
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:51,360
to take care of the family that you're caring for.

152
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:54,320
So that's not a simple thing that he's asking him to do.

153
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,820
So the young man, the one thing that is recorded in all three Gospels

154
00:09:57,820 --> 00:10:02,000
is that the young man walked away sad and that he was a rich man.

155
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,780
That's the important bit that all three harp on, right?

156
00:10:04,780 --> 00:10:08,520
It's a rich guy, he's taking care of these, and he walked away sad.

157
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:09,300
Why was he sad?

158
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:10,200
Had he lost anything?

159
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:12,860
Does he have to give away his money that he's worried about?

160
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:14,560
No, he's not lost his money.

161
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:15,220
Why is he sad?

162
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:18,040
Well, because he finds that he can't inherit in the kingdom

163
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:21,480
and he's disappointed by how he was addressed by this rabbi.

164
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:26,240
He comes up and he says,

165
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:32,100
good teacher, how, what must I do to inherit eternal life?

166
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:37,780
And as he says that, there's an inherent link he's created already

167
00:10:37,780 --> 00:10:42,200
baked into his question that I think goodness is going to be tied to inheritance.

168
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:47,140
If I'm good enough, I should be qualified to inherit.

169
00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:52,420
So the good teacher, and Christ sees that instantly, and he says to him, he addresses that instantly

170
00:10:52,420 --> 00:10:53,920
by saying, why are you calling me good?

171
00:10:53,920 --> 00:10:58,640
He calls him out, giving him the opportunity to say, there's none good but God, is what Jesus

172
00:10:58,640 --> 00:10:59,120
says to him.

173
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:03,940
So that's giving the rich young ruler the opportunity to either say, oh, well, then you must be God,

174
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:07,700
or to say, oh, I understand it's not about goodness, right?

175
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:08,820
Either way, he could have gotten that.

176
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:12,840
But what he actually got from it was he was told not to say good teacher, so he doesn't.

177
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:15,520
When he addresses him the second time, he just says, well, teacher, right?

178
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:19,340
Which is what we get from this is the young man was very, he was a good student.

179
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:20,300
He paid attention.

180
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:22,100
He was trying to do what he was told to do.

181
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,560
And Christ saw this in him.

182
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:27,500
So he has addressed him and said, have you followed the commandments?

183
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:29,420
And does he list all 10 commandments?

184
00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:30,740
He does not.

185
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:36,100
He lists five or six of them, depending on which Matthew, Mark, or Luke you're looking at.

186
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:41,420
And he lists the ones out there that are about how you treat your neighbor, right?

187
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:48,060
Don't murder, don't commit adultery, don't steal, don't lie about him in court, don't commit

188
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:50,840
fraud, honor your father and mother, that's six.

189
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:57,940
So these are the ones that he's asking him, when he said, how am I to inherit in the kingdom

190
00:11:57,940 --> 00:11:58,340
of God?

191
00:11:58,340 --> 00:11:59,540
How do I get eternal life?

192
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:02,200
Christ said, how are you treating your neighbor, right?

193
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:09,520
And tellingly, in Matthew, he also adds one more that wouldn't have made sense to the Roman

194
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:14,520
audiences, so it makes sense that that was omitted from the Mark and Luke list.

195
00:12:14,520 --> 00:12:20,860
But in Matthew, it also says, love your neighbor as yourself.

196
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:26,460
So I'm willing to believe that he had not murdered anyone, given the benefit of the doubt, that

197
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:28,280
he hasn't committed adultery, he hasn't committed fraud.

198
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:31,760
When he says, I've done all things for my youth up, that seems reasonable.

199
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:36,120
It's possible, though, that Christ is pushing him a little further in trying to get him to

200
00:12:36,120 --> 00:12:39,040
say, how do you treat those, your neighbors in society?

201
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:46,060
And we know from elsewhere that Christ believes the first and greatest commandment is Deuteronomy

202
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:49,180
6, love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind, and strength, and love your neighbor

203
00:12:49,180 --> 00:12:53,100
as yourself, he says, is the second commandment, and much like the first.

204
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:57,100
So loving your God and loving your neighbor are closely tied, but loving your neighbor comes

205
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:58,120
from Leviticus 19.

206
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:04,040
It's this, if you go look it up, it's a laundry list, just sort of a scattershot of seemingly

207
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:05,940
disparate commandments, just in a list.

208
00:13:05,940 --> 00:13:11,740
And it should have, it would have struck many people at the time as odd that Christ would

209
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:15,080
have pulled just one of those out as the second and greatest commandment.

210
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:20,580
But in Leviticus 19, it begins, you shall be holy for I, the Lord God, am holy.

211
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:23,680
So this is a list of how to be holy, how to be set apart for God's purposes.

212
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:29,120
And it starts, if you look at the theme, you read the list a few times, you'll see what

213
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:34,080
it is, is a list of how to be kind to those, how to be neighborly to those, how to be, it

214
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:39,600
says, godly to those, how to be holy around those that culture doesn't value.

215
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:45,640
So it begins the list with the first thing, leave your gleaning for the poor and unprotected

216
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:47,100
when you're farming and you drop something.

217
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:49,340
You have to leave it there for the poor people to pick up, right?

218
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:51,520
God's saying, that's how you become holy.

219
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:52,800
I am the Lord, your God.

220
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:59,040
It goes on to say, don't swear my name, don't swear falsely, don't sort of lie and say that

221
00:13:59,040 --> 00:13:59,940
God's on your side.

222
00:13:59,940 --> 00:14:04,380
Don't rob your neighbor, pay your laborers as soon as you can.

223
00:14:04,380 --> 00:14:08,200
If you have day laborers working for you that are working hand to mouth, you pay them immediately.

224
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:12,080
You don't kind of keep the interest on the money and pay them when it's convenient for you.

225
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:14,600
You look out for those who need the money now.

226
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:16,600
I am the Lord.

